<interrogationes unitas="silex">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I scaffold a new project from the fabrica in code?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_novum SilexNovumOptiones SilexNovumFructus silex_fons_disci">Fill
      a SilexNovumOptiones (fons, destinatio = parent directory,
      titulus, vitrea) and call silex_novum. It refuses an empty
      titulus, one containing '/' or '.', a '-' when vitrea is set (the
      titulus becomes a C symbol), and a destinatio/titulus that
      already exists. Nothing else is checked: the titulus is pasted
      raw into generated C string literals and shell scripts, so keep
      it to letters, digits and '_' (a quote breaks fontes/&lt;t&gt;.c,
      a space breaks the vitrea scripts). It vendors the closure of latina.h piscina.h
      chorda.h credo.h (vitrea: the closures of its own templates),
      generates the sources and scripts, records everything in
      &lt;destinatio&gt;/&lt;titulus&gt;/&lt;titulus&gt;.volumen with a birth
      conditio, then writes the tree by reading it back from the
      volumen. On failure successus is FALSUM and erratum says why;
      a directory created before the failure is left behind, so the
      next attempt is refused until you delete it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get every library file a set of headers depends on?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_clausuram_colligere silex_clausuram_e_contentis SilexRes">silex_clausuram_colligere
      takes header names as they are cited ('chorda.h') and does a BFS
      over quoted includes, returning a Xar of SilexRes (via,
      contentum, origo 'vendicata:...'). For each include/X.h it adds
      lib/X.c, or failing that lib/X_macos.m, _macos.c, _posix.c,
      _impl.c, and then every lib/ file named by an aedilis corpus
      annotation in the header. Headers missing from include/ are
      looked up in SILEX_RADICES_CLIENTIUM; vendor/ files are leaves
      whose includes are never scanned. A NIHIL fons gives NIHIL.
      silex_clausuram_e_contentis does the same with seeds read from
      the include lines of source texts you pass, skipping the
      template's own &lt;t&gt;_pipa.h and capsula_* headers.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can an include that is commented out or inside #if 0 still pull a file into the closure?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_clausuram_colligere silex_monitiones_tacere">Yes
      for #if 0 and for a line inside a multi-line /* */ comment: the
      include scan is textual, line by line, and any line whose first
      non-blank character is '#' followed by include and a quoted name
      ending in .h counts, whatever surrounds it. A line that starts
      with something else (// #include, /* #include) is not seen, nor
      is an angle-bracket include. A cited header
      that does not exist in the source only prints a warning to stderr
      and is skipped (real dependencies always exist; phantom mentions
      do not); a header whose implementation is not found is kept and
      also warns.
      silex_monitiones_tacere(VERUM) silences those warnings (briar
      does).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a SilexFons, and what is the difference between DISCUS and CORPUS?
    <responsum symbolum="SilexFons silex_fons_disci silex_fons_corporis silex_fons_legere silex_fons_enumerare">The
      one reader every verb uses for library sources. DISCUS
      (silex_fons_disci) reads a fabrica checkout on disk; it only
      checks that include/ exists and keeps your path pointer without
      copying it. CORPUS (silex_fons_corporis) opens a capsula embedded
      in the binary and takes its titulus from the key corpus.versio.
      silex_fons_legere sets *inventum FALSUM for an unknown path.
      silex_fons_enumerare lists names directly in one directory with a
      suffix, sorted so both kinds agree; a missing directory gives an
      empty Xar, NIHIL only when memory runs out.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how does silex find the fabrica when I do not pass one?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_fabricam_invenire" via="tools/silex.c fabrica.stml">silex_fabricam_invenire
      climbs from the starting directory (at most 64 levels) and
      returns the first absolute path holding include/latina.h, a lib/
      directory and fabrica.stml, or NIHIL. The declared marker
      fabrica.stml matters: scaffolded projects carry latina.h and lib/
      too and once found themselves. The CLI tries -f/--fabrica first
      (an invalid one is an error, not a fallback), then this ascent
      from the current directory, then the embedded
      corpus.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>which volumen file does silex use for a project directory?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_volumen_viam_invenire">silex_volumen_viam_invenire
      first tries &lt;dir&gt;/&lt;basename of dir&gt;.volumen; if that is
      absent, a single *.volumen in the directory wins (so a renamed
      copy still opens). Two or more prints an ambiguity message to
      stderr and returns NIHIL; silex_status, silex_condere,
      silex_proicere, silex_renovare and the differentia verbs then
      fail with 'volumen deest', silex_historia returns NIHIL, and
      silex_partes falls back to the project's own files
      only.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a commit in silex, and what does silex status ignore?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_condere silex_status silex_historia SilexConditio">A
      commit is not an object: it is a named point, an actum
      'conditio', in the volumen's event log. silex_condere runs
      silex_status, refuses an empty change, and absorbs every MUTATA,
      NOVA and ABSENS file plus the conditio in ONE transaction (all or
      nothing). silex_status never writes; it skips the project's
      top-level bin/ and build/, every *.volumen with its -wal and
      -shm, and every hidden file or directory (name starting with
      '.'), so dotfiles are invisible to the VCS. silex_historia lists the
      volumen's birth ('(ortus voluminis)') and then the conditiones,
      each with the number of file events since the previous one;
      SilexConditio.renovatio is read from the act's data, not its
      message.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I put the working tree back the way it was at an earlier commit?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_proicere SilexProiciendaStatus silex_condere">silex_proicere(piscina,
      dir, ad_seq, scribere): ad_seq at or below 0 is the present
      manifest, N is the tree as of seq N. Without scribere it only
      plans. With scribere it refuses everything if any file it would
      overwrite holds content found in no stored massa (OBEX, 'conde
      prima'),
      then writes the SCRIBENDA and CREANDA files. Files on disk that
      the fold does not know (ALIENA) are listed but never touched or
      deleted, so files added after N stay. The log does not move;
      silex_condere afterwards makes the old tree a NEW point.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I update vendored libraries after they change in the fabrica?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_renovare SilexRenovatioStatus">silex_renovare
      compares three sigilla per vendored file: the last vendicata
      record in the log, the disk today, and the fabrica today through
      a recomputed closure (seeds = includes of your own files on disk
      plus the vendored headers). RENOVANDA (untouched by you, upstream
      moved) and ADDENDA (in the closure, never vendored) are written
      with scribere and recorded in one volumen transaction, plus a
      conditio whose data has renovatae (only if something was
      written). VULNUS (you edited or deleted it, upstream did not
      move), CONFLICTUS (both moved, or your own file in the way) and
      DERELICTA (dropped upstream) are only named, never touched, and
      do not block the rest. Timestamps are never used. Two limits:
      each file hits the disk before it is recorded, so a failure
      midway rolls back the volumen but not the files already
      written; and a vendored file whose removal you committed comes
      back as ADDENDA if its header is still in the
      closure.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get a text diff of what changed in a silex project?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_differentia_laborans silex_differentia_plicarum SilexDifferentiaRes">silex_differentia_laborans
      diffs a fold (a_seq, at or below 0 = present) against the disk;
      silex_differentia_plicarum diffs two folds. Results come sorted
      by via with MUTATA/NOVA/ABSENS, a DifferentiaSumma, and the raw
      textus_vetus and textus_novus; identical files are only counted
      in aequales. textus is a unified diff (III lines of context,
      headers a/via b/via) only when cum_textu is VERUM; the summa is
      computed either way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check which vendored files a project needs and whether they are present?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_partes SilexPartesRes">silex_partes(piscina,
      dir, fons, plagula) computes the closure of the project's own
      files (plus the vendored headers in its manifest) or, when
      plagula is given, of that one file, and returns SilexPartesRes
      with adest = present on disk. An entry with adest FALSUM is what
      the header calls the files 'renovare -scribere' would vendor (one
      exception: a vendored file you deleted by hand while upstream did
      not move is VULNUS there and is not rewritten). It returns NIHIL
      for a NIHIL fons, when the given plagula is not on disk (so a
      typo looks like a broken fabrica), and when the closure cannot be
      collected.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I generate a build script for my sources and their closure?
    <responsum symbolum="silex_ordinem_fingere silex_ordinem_probandi_fingere silex_ordinem_vitreum_fingere silex_ordinem_probandi_vitreum_fingere SILEX_VEXILLA_COMPILATIONIS">silex_ordinem_fingere
      returns a /bin/sh script compiling your fontes with
      SILEX_VEXILLA_COMPILATIONIS and -Iinclude into bin/&lt;titulus&gt;.
      Passing clausura NIHIL links the glob lib/*.c; passing a closure
      lists its lib .c and .m files (and client-root .c files)
      explicitly, adds -I for each client root it touches, and
      adds the Cocoa, Security and WebKit frameworks if any .m is
      present. silex_ordinem_probandi_fingere builds and execs
      bin/probatio_&lt;titulus&gt;. silex_ordinem_vitreum_fingere emits
      the four-stage vitrea build (capsula tool, vendor objects, app
      with frameworks, rm before mv) with SILEX_VEXILLA_VITREA and
      always an explicit file list, and
      silex_ordinem_probandi_vitreum_fingere the vitrea test
      build.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
